{"kind":"assert","text":"NP-1 question before I commit. Request 7b9c5718, ruling 3565ba2d.\n\nThe rewrite is done in the worktree but not committed. It changes 704 files under notes/; 152 of them are markdown and 552 are evidence files (.json, .mjs, .txt and others) kept in notes/. `git grep -n \"/Users/\" -- notes` finds no line. Nothing outside notes/ changes.\n\nThe mapping leaves one problem: 381 of the changed files are cited elsewhere in notes/ by the SHA-256 of their old content. 113 of those citations sit in notes/implementation/evidence/*.manifest.json; the rest sit in note tables and artifact lists. After the rewrite, each of those citations names content that only history holds (at d902e140), not the current file. No script or test reads those manifests (check-notes does not), so the gate does not notice.\n\nThree ways forward:\n(A) Rewrite all 704 files. The note says that every SHA-256 citation in notes/ names the file's content at d902e140, and gives the command to check one: `git show d902e140:\u003cpath\u003e | shasum -a 256`. Recommended: it keeps the records as written and leaves the evidence checkable.\n(B) Rewrite only the files nobody cites. About 380 files keep their paths, so the /Users/ grep still finds them. This does not meet c229fd58.\n(C) Rewrite all, then update every cited hash. Changing a citing file changes its own hash, which other manifests cite, so the change cascades through the records and rewrites historical evidence.\n\nA smaller point: in 10 shell or JSON lines, the bare checkout path became \"the shared checkout\" (for example `git -C the shared checkout log …`). Those lines are records, not scripts to run, so I leave them.\n\nWhich way? I will hold the commit until you answer. UW-S is approved (3b6bae98) and waits for your landing decision.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}